{"translation":"Bidding farewell to a guest by night on the Xunyang River bank,
Maple leaves and reed flowers rustle in the autumn chill.
I dismount my horse while he boards his boat;
We raise our wine, but lack flutes and strings.
Drunken yet joyless, we part in sorrow;
As we bid goodbye, the vast river soaks the moon.
Suddenly, from across the water, comes the sound of a pipa;
I forget to return home, and he delays setting sail.
Following the notes, I quietly ask who plays;
The music stops, as if hesitant to speak.
I move my boat closer and invite her out;
More wine is poured, lamps are lit again, and the feast resumes.
After countless urging calls, she finally emerges,
Still holding the pipa, half-covering her face.
She turns the pegs and plucks two or three strings;
Before the melody forms, emotion already flows.
Each string suppresses sighs within each note,
Seeming to tell of a lifetime unfulfilled.
Head lowered, fingers glide casually across the strings,
Confiding all the boundless matters of her heart.
Light sweeps, slow twists, wipes and picks;
First "Rainbow Robe," then "Six Strings" follow.
The heavy strings roar like sudden rain;
The thin strings whisper like private talk.
Roaring and whispering intertwined in play,
Like large and small pearls falling onto a jade plate.
Bright birdsong glides smoothly under blossoms;
