**The Song of the Pipa**

By the Xunyang River, seeing a guest off at night;
Maple leaves and reed flowers shiver in the autumn chill.
I dismount from my horse while he remains aboard his boat;
We raise our cups to drink, but lack the music’s charm.
Drunkenness brings no joy; sorrow marks our parting;
And when we bid farewell, the moon swims in the vast, misty river.

Suddenly, over the water, I hear the sound of a pipa.
The host forgets to return home; the guest delays his departure.
Following the sound, I quietly ask who is playing.
The pipa notes cease; she hesitates, reluctant to speak.
I move my boat closer and invite her to meet me;
More wine is poured, lamps are trimmed, and the banquet resumes.
After many calls and invitations, she finally emerges,
Still holding her pipa, half-covering her face.

She turns the pegs and plucks the strings two or three times;
Before the melody forms, emotion already flows.
Each string suppressed, each note filled with thought,
As if telling of a lifetime spent in frustration.
She lowers her brow and plays continuously,
Expressing all the boundless matters of her heart.
Lightly pressing, slowly twisting, brushing and picking—
First playing "Rainbow Robes," then later "Six Strings."

The thick strings rumble like sudden heavy rain;
The thin strings whisper like private murmurs.
Rumbling and whispering intertwine as she plays;
Big pearls and small pearls drop onto a jade plate.
Melodious like orioles singing amidst blooming flowers;
Choked sobs like spring water flowing under ice, difficult.
The cold, sluggish spring freezes the strings into silence;
When the flow stops, the sound temporarily ceases.

A hidden melancholy and unspoken resentment arise;
At this moment, silence surpasses sound.
Then, like a silver vase suddenly bursting, water spurts out;
Like armored cavalry charging forth, swords and spears clash.
The tune ends; she draws the pick across the center strings;
One final sharp sound tears silk apart.

East and west boats fall silent; no one speaks.
Only the white autumn moon shines upon the river’s heart.
She pauses in deep thought, inserts the pick between the strings,
Straightens her dress, rises, and composes her expression.
She says: "Originally, I was a girl from the capital;
My home lies beneath the Toad Hill.
At thirteen, I mastered the pipa;
My name ranked first among the Imperial Music Bureau.
After finishing a piece, even great masters admired me;
Once made up, every beauty envied my grace.
Young men of the Five Tombs vied to give me gifts;
For one song, countless bolts of red chiffon were thrown.
Gold-headed hairpins snapped keeping time;
Blood-red skirts stained by spilled wine.
Year after year, laughter repeated itself;
Autumn moons and spring breezes passed casually.
My brother went to war; my aunt died;
Evenings fade, mornings come, and beauty fades.
Before my door, desolate; horses and carriages scarce.
Growing old, I married a merchant’s wife.
Merchants value profit but neglect separation;
Last month, he went to Fuliang to buy tea.
He left; I stayed at the river mouth guarding an empty boat;
Around the ship, bright moonlight; below, cold waters.
Deep in the night, I suddenly dreamed of youthful days;
In the dream, I wept; makeup mixed with tears streaked reddish lines."

Hearing her pipa, I had already sighed heavily;
Now hearing these words, I sigh even deeper.
"We are both lost souls drifting at the end of the world;
Having met here, why did we need to know each other before?
Since last year, I left the Imperial Capital,
Banished, lying ill in Xunyang City.
Xunyang is remote, devoid of music;
All year long, not a sound of silk or bamboo is heard.
Living near the Pen River, the land is low and damp;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo surround my house.
What do I hear here, morning and evening?
Cuckoos bleeding their cries; apes wailing sadly.
On spring river nights under flowers, on autumn moonlit evenings,
Often I take wine alone and pour it down myself.
Are there no mountain songs and village flutes?
Their hoarse, discordant sounds are hard to bear.
Tonight, hearing your pipa language,
It is like listening to immortal music; my ears clear momentarily.
Do not refuse to sit again and play one more tune;
For you, I shall compose 'The Ballad of the Pipa.'"

Moved by my words, she stood for a long time.
Returning to her seat, she tightened the strings; the tempo grew urgent.
Mournful tones, unlike those played before;
Everyone present covered their faces and wept anew.
Who in the audience shed the most tears?
The Sima of Jiangzhou’s blue robe was wet.

By the Yangtze at Xunyang, I saw off a guest by night;
Maple leaves and reed flowers shivered in autumn’s blight.
I dismounted, my guest remained aboard his boat;
We raised our cups, but no music played to soothe our throat.
Drunk yet joyless, we faced parting’s sorrowful tide;
Moonlight drowned in the vast river, spreading far and wide.
Suddenly, a pipa’s voice rose from the water’s surface;
The host forgot to leave, the guest delayed his passage.

Seeking its source, I asked softly: who is playing?
The pipa stopped; she seemed hesitant, delaying her saying.
I moved my boat closer, inviting her to appear;
More wine was poured, lamps trimmed, the banquet resumed here.
After countless calls, she finally emerged from hiding,
Still cradling the pipa, half-covering her face with shading.
She tuned the pegs, plucked strings two or three times lightly;
Before the melody formed, emotion flowed profusely.
Each string suppressed deep sighs of longing and regret;
As if telling all failures life had ever met.
With lowered brows, fingers strummed on steadily,
Pouring out endless stories from within secretly.

Lightly pressing, slowly twisting, sweeping and picking fast;
First "Rainbow Skirt," then "Six Beats" notes were cast.
The thick strings thundered like sudden rain pouring down;
The thin strings whispered like lovers’ quiet sound.
Thunderous whispers intertwined, complex and bold;
Like pearls large and small falling onto jade plates cold.
Clear as orioles singing beneath blossoms bright;
Muffled as spring streams struggling under ice tight.
Ice-cold, rough, the strings froze, stopping their flow;
Silence hung heavy, where words could not go.
Hidden sorrows and secret resentments arose anew;
This silence surpassed any sound of true hue.
Then suddenly, like silver vases bursting apart;
Iron cavalry charged, swords clashing, spears start.
When the song ended, she drew the pick across center;
All four strings snapped once, tearing silk’s tender texture.
Boats east and west fell silent, awe-struck and still;
Only the white autumn moon reflected on the rill.

She paused, put away the pick among the strings;
Straightened her robes, composed herself with dignified things.
She said she was originally from the capital’s grandeur;
Her home near Xiama Ling, amidst elegance splendor.
At thirteen, mastered pipa art with grace untold;
Ranked first among performers of the Imperial fold.
After performances, skilled masters praised her skill;
Completed makeup stirred jealousy, beauty’s thrill.
Young nobles of Five Tombs competed for gifts thrown;
For one song, red silk given, number unknown.
Gold-inlaid combs broken keeping time’s beat;
Blood-red skirts stained by spilled liquor’s heat.
Laughing and rejoicing year after year passed;
Autumn moons and spring breezes, carelessly wasted.
Brother joined army, aunt died in sudden fate;
Time flew, beauty aged, left desolate.
Door became quiet, horses sparse and few;
Aged too long, married a merchant, bound by duty’s cue.
Merchants value profit over love’s farewell pain;
Last month he went to Fuliang to trade tea again.
Gone, she waited alone at river mouth’s shore;
Around her boat, moonlight bright, water cold once more.
Deep in night dreams, youth memories flooded back;
Crying in dream, tears ruined makeup’s black.

Hearing her pipa, I sighed deeply in soul;
Hearing these words, grief overwhelmed me whole.
We are both wanderers fallen from high estate;
Meeting now, why need past acquaintance wait?
Since last year I left the imperial city’s gate;
Exiled, ill, living in Xunyang’s remote state.
Xunyang is isolated, lacking musical tune;
No string or bamboo sounds heard throughout noon.
Near Pengjiang River, land low, damp, and wet;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboos around us set.
What do I hear morning and evening in this place?
Cuckoos bleeding cries, apes wailing in disgrace.
Spring rivers, flower mornings, autumn moonlit nights;
I often take wine, drinking alone in plight.
Are there no mountain songs or village flute’s call?
Rough, harsh noises, unbearable and pall.
Tonight I hear your pipa’s language sweet;
Like celestial music, ears made complete.
Please sit again, play another piece for me;
To compose "Song of the Pipa," inspired by thee.

Moved by my words, she stood still for a while long;
Returned to seat, tightened strings, played urgent song.
Sorrowful tones unlike previous gentle plea;
Entire audience wept, covered faces tearfully.
Who shed most tears among those gathered near?
Jiangzhou’s Prefect, blue robe soaked with fear.

messages.content:
At the mouth of the Xunyang River, I bid farewell to a guest by night;
Maple leaves and reed flowers rustled in the autumn chill.
The host dismounted, while the guest remained aboard his boat;
We raised our cups to drink, but there were no pipes or strings to play.
Drunk yet joyless, we parted in sorrow;
As we left, the vast river was steeped in moonlight.

Suddenly, from over the water, came the sound of a pipa;
The host forgot to return home, and the guest delayed his departure.
Tracing the sound, I asked discreetly who was playing;
The music ceased, as if she wished to speak but hesitated.
I moved my boat closer to invite her out;
More wine was poured, lamps were lit, and the feast resumed.

After countless calls and entreaties, she finally emerged,
Still holding her pipa, half-concealing her face.
She turned the pegs and plucked the strings two or three times;
Before forming any melody, her emotion was already present.

Every string concealed restraint, every note harbored thought,
Seeming to tell of a life unfulfilled and ambitions thwarted.
With lowered brows, she played on without pause,
Expressing all the infinite affairs within her heart.

Lightly gathering, slowly twisting, brushing then picking—
First "Rainbow Robes," then "Six Flutes" followed.
The thick strings sounded loud like sudden rain;
The thin strings whispered soft like private talk.
Loud and soft intertwined in complex harmony,
Like large and small pearls falling into a plate of jade.

Now smooth as orioles singing through flowers below,
Now choked as springs struggling beneath ice.
Ice-cold waters slowed, strings seemed frozen still;
When music failed to flow, sound momentarily ceased.

Then arose hidden sorrow and secret resentment;
In that silence, quiet spoke louder than words.
Silver bottles burst with gushing spray;
Iron cavalry surged forth, swords and spears clashing.

The song ended; she drew the pick across the center strings;
One sharp sound ripped like tearing silk.
Boats to the east and ships to the west fell silent;
Only the white autumn moon hung over the river’s heart.

Lost in deep thought, she inserted her pick among the strings,
Straightened her clothes and rose, composing her features.
She said: "Once I was a woman of the capital city,
My home lay near Shrimp Mound Ridge.
At thirteen, I mastered the pipa;
My name belonged to the First Division of the Imperial Troupe.
When I finished a piece, even skilled masters admired me;
When dressed up, beautiful courtesans envied my grace.

Young men of the Five Tombs competed to toss me red silk scarves;
For a single song, I received countless bolts of fine fabric.
Jeweled hairpins broke keeping time to the beat;
Blood-red skirts spilled with stains of wine.
Year after year passed in laughter and revelry;
Autumn moons and spring winds were spent idly away.

Then my brother went off to join the army, and my aunt died;
Day by day, youth faded from my face.
The door before me grew cold; horses and carriages became scarce;
Aging, I married a merchant for a wife’s status.

He values profit above all else, indifferent to separation;
Last month he went to Fuliang to buy tea.
Gone and gone, I guard an empty boat at the river mouth;
Around the vessel, bright moonlight reflects on chilling waters.

Deep in the night, I suddenly dreamt of youthful days;
Crying in my dreams, tears stained my makeup red."

Hearing the pipa, I had already sighed deeply;
Listening to these words, I moaned again in grief.
We are both down-and-out wanderers under heaven’s wide sky;
Meeting now, why bother wondering if we knew each other before?

Since last year, having left the Emperor’s capital,
I have been exiled, lying ill in Xunyang City.
Xunyang is remote, devoid of music;
All year long, I hear not the sound of丝竹 (strings and flutes).
Living near the Pen River, where land is low and damp,
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo grow around my dwelling.

What do I hear here, morning and evening?
Dusky cuckoos crying blood, apes wailing mournfully.
On spring river mornings, flower-filled dawns, autumn moonlit nights,
Often I take wine and pour it down alone.

Are there no mountain songs and village whistles?
Their harsh, grating sounds are painful to the ear.
Tonight, hearing your pipa melodies,
It is like listening to celestial music; my ears are temporarily cleared.

Please do not refuse to sit again and play one more tune;
For your sake, I shall compose the *Song of the Pipa*.

Moved by my words, she stood for a long time;
Then sat down, tightened the strings, which grew urgent once more.
Sorrowful tones unlike those heard before;
Everyone in the room listened again, covering their faces in tears.

Who among us shed the most tears?
The Blue-Robed Magistrate of Jiangzhou, whose robe was wet.

