译文：**The Ballad of the Pipa**

On the banks of the Xunyan River, I bid a friend farewell at night;
Maple leaves and reed flowers shiver in the chill of autumn light.
I dismount my horse while he stays upon his boat;
We raise our cups to drink, but no music lifts our throat.
Drunk yet joyless, sorrow shadows our parting soon;
As we depart, vast waters hold the moon’s pale tune.
Suddenly, from above the water, pipa strings begin to sing;
The host forgets to return, the guest delays his setting.
Following the sound, I ask who plays in hidden grace;
The pipa pauses, hesitant to show its face.
I move my boat close by to invite her sight;
More wine is poured, lamps turned up high for the night.
Called out many times, she finally appears,
Still holding her pipa, half-hiding tears.
She turns the pegs and plucks the strings, just two or three sounds;
Before the melody forms, emotion already bounds.
Each string suppressed, each note deep with thought,
Seems to tell of a life where hopes were caught.
Head bowed low, fingers moving free and slow,
Expressing all the secrets that her heart must know.
Lightly gathering, slowly twisting, wiping then picking clear;
First “Rainbow Skirt,” then “Six Strings” bring the song near.
The thick strings thunder like a sudden rainstorm’s roar;
The thin strings whisper intimate tales before.
Roaring and whispering, intertwined they play;
Like pearls large and small dropping onto jade trays.
Sweet as orioles singing under blossom’s shade;
Choked like springs flowing beneath ice blockade.
Ice-chill, cold and stiff, the strings seem frozen still;
Frozen silence falls, the music takes its fill.
Then suddenly arises hidden grief and secret hate;
In this silent moment, quiet wins over fate.
A silver vase bursts open, water splashes forth;
Iron cavalry charges, swords clash in their worth.
When the song ends, she draws the plectrum across the center;
One final strum on four strings, like silk torn thinner.
East boats and west barges fall into silent awe;
Only the white moon in the river’s heart they saw.
She sits in contemplation, sticks the pick between the strings;
Adjusts her clothes, composes herself, dignity clings.
She says: “Originally I was a girl of the capital town;
My home lay beneath Shrimp-Frog Hill, renowned.”
“At thirteen years old, I mastered the pipa’s art;
My name ranked first among the imperial court’s part.”
“After playing a piece, even great masters would bow;
When done with makeup, beautiful Qiuniang envied how.”
“Young men of Five Tombs vied to give me silk;
For one song played, bolts of red gauze beyond reck.”
“Gold-inlaid hairpins broke keeping time’s beat;
Blood-red skirts stained by overturned wine’s heat.”
“This year laughs again next year, joy renewed;
Autumn moon and spring breeze pass, life viewed crude.”
“My brother went to war, my aunt died in bed;
Evenings go, mornings come, beauty fled.”
“My door grew cold, horses rare at the gate;
Old now, I married a merchant, bound by fate.”
“The merchant values profit, cares not for goodbye;
Last month to Fuliang he went, tea to buy.”
“He left and returned, guarding empty ship at mouth;
Around the boat, bright moon, cold river south.”
“In deep night, suddenly dreams of youthful days;
Crying in dream, rouge tears stain the lace.”
Hearing her pipa, I sighed with heavy heart;
Hearing these words, I groan in deep apart.
Both are fallen souls wandering far away;
Why need we have known each other prior day?
Since last year leaving the Imperial City walls;
Banished here sickly, within these damp halls.
Xunyan is remote, without music’s cheer;
All year long, no silk or bamboo to hear.
Living near Pen River, land low and wet;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo around us set.
What do we hear at dawn and dusk’s decree?
Dusky cuckoos bleeding cries, gibbon’s plea.
Spring rivers, flower mornings, autumn moon nights;
Often take wine alone, pouring delights.
Are there no mountain songs or village flute?
Rough and harsh sounds, difficult to suit.
Tonight hearing your pipa’s spoken tongue;
Like listening to celestial music, ears young.
Do not refuse to sit and play another song;
For you, I write “The Ballad of the Pipa” strong.
Moved by my words, she stood long in place;
Returned to seat, tightened strings, tempo’s pace.
Mournful unlike earlier tunes so bright;
Seat full once more, all weeping in night.
Who sheds the most tears among those present?
Sima of Jiangzhou’s blue robe, soaked and tense.

message.content:
Night by the Xunyang River, I see off a guest;
Maple leaves and reed blossoms rustle in autumn's breath.
The host dismounts, his friend is on the boat to stay;
We raise our cups, yet lack music to brighten the day.
Drunk but not joyful, grief rises at parting time;
The vast river swallows the moon in silent rhyme.
Suddenly, from across the water, pipa sounds arrive,
The host forgets home, the guest delays departure alive.

Seeking the source, I ask who plays in the dim night;
The pipa pauses, hesitant to speak out of sight.
I move my boat closer, inviting her to appear,
Replenish the wine, relight lamps, banishing fear.
After thousand calls and ten thousand pleas she emerges,
Still holding the pipa, half-hiding her face with verges.

She tunes the pegs, plucks strings—two or three notes ring;
Before the melody forms, emotion already springs.
Each string suppresses sighs, each sound carries thought;
As if telling life's disappointments, plainly sought.
Head lowered, fingers wander freely, playing on;
Unspoken depths of heart are fully revealed in song.

Lightly gathering, slowly twisting, sweeping then picking;
First "Rainbow Skirt," then "Six Strings," melodies thickening.
The big strings thunder like heavy rain pouring down;
The small strings whisper soft as private love's own sound.
Thuddering and whispering interweave and mix;
Like pearls large and small falling into jade dishes' tricks.

Chirping orioles glide smooth beneath floral canopies;
Muffled sobs like spring water choked under icy barriers.
Ice-cold, sluggish, strings freeze, sound stops abruptly;
Silence speaks louder than any note explicitly.

Suddenly, silver vase bursts, water spurts high;
Iron cavalry charges, swords clash, cries fill the sky.
At the end, she draws the plectrum across the center;
Four strings snap like tearing silk—a sharp crescendo tender.

East boats and west barges fall into quiet awe;
Only the white moon in the river reflects raw law.
She hesitates, places the plectrum 'twixt the strings;
Straightens her clothes, composes herself, dignity brings.

"I was once a maiden of the capital city;
My home lay near Shrimp Hill, where memories sit pretty.
At thirteen, I mastered the pipa's art so well;
Named first among the academy's bells that fell.

After songs, skilled masters bowed to my command;
When dressed up, Autumn Lady envied my hand.
Young nobles of Five Tombs fought for me with gifts;
One tune brought countless red silks, no count it shifts.

Gold-inlaid hairpins broke keeping the beat true;
Blood-red skirts stained with spilled wine, a youthful view.
Year after year laughter echoed, carefree days passed;
Autumn moon and spring breeze wasted, nothing held fast.

Brother joined the army, aunt died in sorrow's wake;
Evenings fade, mornings come, beauty wears thin and break.
Before my door, horses rare, attention cold now;
Older, I married a merchant, taking my vow.

He prizes profit over farewell's gentle tear;
Last month to Fuliang he went, buying tea there.
Going and coming, guarding empty boat at mouth;
Around the ship, bright moon shines, cold river south.

Deep in night, dreams of youth return unbidden;
Waking crying, makeup tears trace red lines hidden.

Hearing the pipa, I sighed deeply before;
Hearing these words, again mournful groans pour.
Both we are drifters lost at world's far edge;
Meeting here, why care if old acquaintance pledge?

Since last year I left the Imperial Capital's gate;
Demoted, sick, in Xunyang City lies my fate.
Remote land, no music heard throughout the year;
No strings or pipes sound, silence deep and sheer.

Living near Pen River, ground low and wet;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo surround us yet.
What do I hear morning and evening in this place?
Cuckoos cry blood, gibbons moan in lament's embrace.

Spring river, flower mornings, autumn moonlit nights;
Often I take wine, drinking alone in slight.
Are there no folk songs or village flutes to cheer?
Crackling, grating sounds hard for ear to hear.

Tonight hearing your pipa speech, divine music clear;
My ears brightened, listening to celestial sphere.
Do not refuse to sit and play another tune;
For you, I'll compose this Ballad of the Pipa soon.

Moved by my words, she stood long in silence deep;
Then sat back, tightened strings, tempo sped leap.
Sorrowful tones unlike those before, more keen;
Full house heard again, all hiding faces unseen.

Who weeps most among them? Tears flow in stream?
The Sima of Jiangzhou, blue robe soaked with dream.

