译文：By the Yangtze at Xunyang, I saw off a guest by night;
Maple leaves and reed flowers shivered in autumn’s blight.
I dismounted, my guest remained aboard his boat;
We raised our cups, but no music played to soothe our throat.
Drunk yet joyless, we faced parting’s sorrowful tide;
Moonlight drowned in the vast river, spreading far and wide.
Suddenly, a pipa’s voice rose from the water’s surface;
The host forgot to leave, the guest delayed his passage.

Seeking its source, I asked softly: who is playing?
The pipa stopped; she seemed hesitant, delaying her saying.
I moved my boat closer, inviting her to appear;
More wine was poured, lamps trimmed, the banquet resumed here.
After countless calls, she finally emerged from hiding,
Still cradling the pipa, half-covering her face with shading.
She tuned the pegs, plucked strings two or three times lightly;
Before the melody formed, emotion flowed profusely.
Each string suppressed deep sighs of longing and regret;
As if telling all failures life had ever met.
With lowered brows, fingers strummed on steadily,
Pouring out endless stories from within secretly.

Lightly pressing, slowly twisting, sweeping and picking fast;
First "Rainbow Skirt," then "Six Beats" notes were cast.
The thick strings thundered like sudden rain pouring down;
The thin strings whispered like lovers’ quiet sound.
Thunderous whispers intertwined, complex and bold;
Like pearls large and small falling onto jade plates cold.
Clear as orioles singing beneath blossoms bright;
Muffled as spring streams struggling under ice tight.
Ice-cold, rough, the strings froze, stopping their flow;
Silence hung heavy, where words could not go.
Hidden sorrows and secret resentments arose anew;
This silence surpassed any sound of true hue.
Then suddenly, like silver vases bursting apart;
Iron cavalry charged, swords clashing, spears start.
When the song ended, she drew the pick across center;
All four strings snapped once, tearing silk’s tender texture.
Boats east and west fell silent, awe-struck and still;
Only the white autumn moon reflected on the rill.

She paused, put away the pick among the strings;
Straightened her robes, composed herself with dignified things.
She said she was originally from the capital’s grandeur;
Her home near Xiama Ling, amidst elegance splendor.
At thirteen, mastered pipa art with grace untold;
Ranked first among performers of the Imperial fold.
After performances, skilled masters praised her skill;
Completed makeup stirred jealousy, beauty’s thrill.
Young nobles of Five Tombs competed for gifts thrown;
For one song, red silk given, number unknown.
Gold-inlaid combs broken keeping time’s beat;
Blood-red skirts stained by spilled liquor’s heat.
Laughing and rejoicing year after year passed;
Autumn moons and spring breezes, carelessly wasted.
Brother joined army, aunt died in sudden fate;
Time flew, beauty aged, left desolate.
Door became quiet, horses sparse and few;
Aged too long, married a merchant, bound by duty’s cue.
Merchants value profit over love’s farewell pain;
Last month he went to Fuliang to trade tea again.
Gone, she waited alone at river mouth’s shore;
Around her boat, moonlight bright, water cold once more.
Deep in night dreams, youth memories flooded back;
Crying in dream, tears ruined makeup’s black.

Hearing her pipa, I sighed deeply in soul;
Hearing these words, grief overwhelmed me whole.
We are both wanderers fallen from high estate;
Meeting now, why need past acquaintance wait?
Since last year I left the imperial city’s gate;
Exiled, ill, living in Xunyang’s remote state.
Xunyang is isolated, lacking musical tune;
No string or bamboo sounds heard throughout noon.
Near Pengjiang River, land low, damp, and wet;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboos around us set.
What do I hear morning and evening in this place?
Cuckoos bleeding cries, apes wailing in disgrace.
Spring rivers, flower mornings, autumn moonlit nights;
I often take wine, drinking alone in plight.
Are there no mountain songs or village flute’s call?
Rough, harsh noises, unbearable and pall.
Tonight I hear your pipa’s language sweet;
Like celestial music, ears made complete.
Please sit again, play another piece for me;
To compose "Song of the Pipa," inspired by thee.

Moved by my words, she stood still for a while long;
Returned to seat, tightened strings, played urgent song.
Sorrowful tones unlike previous gentle plea;
Entire audience wept, covered faces tearfully.
Who shed most tears among those gathered near?
Jiangzhou’s Prefect, blue robe soaked with fear.

