仅据原作独立评价这一份英文译文。先核对全文意义、人物与时空关系、意象修辞及文化理解，再比较情绪文体、篇章完整和英语表达；这七方面等权，不使用任何外部评分结果。输出0到100的单个总分数字，不解释，不改写。原作：{"title": "琵琶行", "author": "白居易", "text": "浔阳江头夜送客，枫叶荻花秋瑟瑟。\n主人下马客在船，举酒欲饮无管弦。\n醉不成欢惨将别，别时茫茫江浸月。\n忽闻水上琵琶声，主人忘归客不发。\n寻声暗问弹者谁，琵琶声停欲语迟。\n移船相近邀相见，添酒回灯重开宴。\n千呼万唤始出来，犹抱琵琶半遮面。\n转轴拨弦三两声，未成曲调先有情。\n弦弦掩抑声声思，似诉平生不得志。\n低眉信手续续弹，说尽心中无限事。\n轻拢慢捻抹复挑，初为霓裳后六幺。\n大弦嘈嘈如急雨，小弦切切如私语。\n嘈嘈切切错杂弹，大珠小珠落玉盘。\n间关莺语花底滑，幽咽泉流冰下难。\n冰泉冷涩弦凝绝，凝绝不通声暂歇。\n别有幽愁暗恨生，此时无声胜有声。\n银瓶乍破水浆迸，铁骑突出刀枪鸣。\n曲终收拨当心画，四弦一声如裂帛。\n东船西舫悄无言，唯见江心秋月白。\n沉吟放拨插弦中，整顿衣裳起敛容。\n自言本是京城女，家在虾蟆陵下住。\n十三学得琵琶成，名属教坊第一部。\n曲罢曾教善才服，妆成每被秋娘妒。\n五陵年少争缠头，一曲红绡不知数。\n钿头银篦击节碎，血色罗裙翻酒污。\n今年欢笑复明年，秋月春风等闲度。\n弟走从军阿姨死，暮去朝来颜色故。\n门前冷落鞍马稀，老大嫁作商人妇。\n商人重利轻别离，前月浮梁买茶去。\n去来江口守空船，绕船月明江水寒。\n夜深忽梦少年事，梦啼妆泪红阑干。\n我闻琵琶已叹息，又闻此语重唧唧。\n同是天涯沦落人，相逢何必曾相识！\n我从去年辞帝京，谪居卧病浔阳城。\n浔阳地僻无音乐，终岁不闻丝竹声。\n住近湓江地低湿，黄芦苦竹绕宅生。\n其间旦暮闻何物？杜鹃啼血猿哀鸣。\n春江花朝秋月夜，往往取酒还独倾。\n岂无山歌与村笛？呕哑嘲哳难为听。\n今夜闻君琵琶语，如听仙乐耳暂明。\n莫辞更坐弹一曲，为君翻作琵琶行。\n感我此言良久立，却坐促弦弦转急。\n凄凄不似向前声，满座重闻皆掩泣。\n座中泣下谁最多？江州司马青衫湿。"}
译文：Night by the Xunyang River, I see off a guest;
Maple leaves and reed blossoms rustle in autumn's breath.
The host dismounts, his friend is on the boat to stay;
We raise our cups, yet lack music to brighten the day.
Drunk but not joyful, grief rises at parting time;
The vast river swallows the moon in silent rhyme.
Suddenly, from across the water, pipa sounds arrive,
The host forgets home, the guest delays departure alive.

Seeking the source, I ask who plays in the dim night;
The pipa pauses, hesitant to speak out of sight.
I move my boat closer, inviting her to appear,
Replenish the wine, relight lamps, banishing fear.
After thousand calls and ten thousand pleas she emerges,
Still holding the pipa, half-hiding her face with verges.

She tunes the pegs, plucks strings—two or three notes ring;
Before the melody forms, emotion already springs.
Each string suppresses sighs, each sound carries thought;
As if telling life's disappointments, plainly sought.
Head lowered, fingers wander freely, playing on;
Unspoken depths of heart are fully revealed in song.

Lightly gathering, slowly twisting, sweeping then picking;
First "Rainbow Skirt," then "Six Strings," melodies thickening.
The big strings thunder like heavy rain pouring down;
The small strings whisper soft as private love's own sound.
Thuddering and whispering interweave and mix;
Like pearls large and small falling into jade dishes' tricks.

Chirping orioles glide smooth beneath floral canopies;
Muffled sobs like spring water choked under icy barriers.
Ice-cold, sluggish, strings freeze, sound stops abruptly;
Silence speaks louder than any note explicitly.

Suddenly, silver vase bursts, water spurts high;
Iron cavalry charges, swords clash, cries fill the sky.
At the end, she draws the plectrum across the center;
Four strings snap like tearing silk—a sharp crescendo tender.

East boats and west barges fall into quiet awe;
Only the white moon in the river reflects raw law.
She hesitates, places the plectrum 'twixt the strings;
Straightens her clothes, composes herself, dignity brings.

"I was once a maiden of the capital city;
My home lay near Shrimp Hill, where memories sit pretty.
At thirteen, I mastered the pipa's art so well;
Named first among the academy's bells that fell.

After songs, skilled masters bowed to my command;
When dressed up, Autumn Lady envied my hand.
Young nobles of Five Tombs fought for me with gifts;
One tune brought countless red silks, no count it shifts.

Gold-inlaid hairpins broke keeping the beat true;
Blood-red skirts stained with spilled wine, a youthful view.
Year after year laughter echoed, carefree days passed;
Autumn moon and spring breeze wasted, nothing held fast.

Brother joined the army, aunt died in sorrow's wake;
Evenings fade, mornings come, beauty wears thin and break.
Before my door, horses rare, attention cold now;
Older, I married a merchant, taking my vow.

He prizes profit over farewell's gentle tear;
Last month to Fuliang he went, buying tea there.
Going and coming, guarding empty boat at mouth;
Around the ship, bright moon shines, cold river south.

Deep in night, dreams of youth return unbidden;
Waking crying, makeup tears trace red lines hidden.

Hearing the pipa, I sighed deeply before;
Hearing these words, again mournful groans pour.
Both we are drifters lost at world's far edge;
Meeting here, why care if old acquaintance pledge?

Since last year I left the Imperial Capital's gate;
Demoted, sick, in Xunyang City lies my fate.
Remote land, no music heard throughout the year;
No strings or pipes sound, silence deep and sheer.

Living near Pen River, ground low and wet;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo surround us yet.
What do I hear morning and evening in this place?
Cuckoos cry blood, gibbons moan in lament's embrace.

Spring river, flower mornings, autumn moonlit nights;
Often I take wine, drinking alone in slight.
Are there no folk songs or village flutes to cheer?
Crackling, grating sounds hard for ear to hear.

Tonight hearing your pipa speech, divine music clear;
My ears brightened, listening to celestial sphere.
Do not refuse to sit and play another tune;
For you, I'll compose this Ballad of the Pipa soon.

Moved by my words, she stood long in silence deep;
Then sat back, tightened strings, tempo sped leap.
Sorrowful tones unlike those before, more keen;
Full house heard again, all hiding faces unseen.

Who weeps most among them? Tears flow in stream?
The Sima of Jiangzhou, blue robe soaked with dream.

message.content:
**The Ballad of the Pipa**

By the banks of the Xunyang River, I bid a friend goodnight;
Maple leaves and reed flowers rustled in the autumn chill.
I dismounted from my horse while he stood on his boat;
We raised our wine cups to drink, but had no music to play.
Though drunk with sorrow, joy we could not find as we part;
At the moment of farewell, the moon drowned in the vast river.

Suddenly, across the water, came the sound of a pipa;
Homebound, I forgot to return; my guest held back his departure.
Seeking the source, I asked softly: "Who is playing?"
The pipa music stopped; she hesitated, reluctant to speak.
I moved my boat close by and invited her to meet us;
More wine was poured, the lamps were brought back, and the feast resumed.
After countless calls and entreaties, at last she emerged,
Still holding the pipa, half-covering her face shyly.

She turned the pegs and plucked the strings, two or three notes;
Before the melody formed, emotion was already there.
Each string murmured low, each note sighed in longing,
As if telling of a lifetime of unfulfilled ambition.
She lowered her brows and played casually, continuing on,
Expressing all the boundless affairs within her heart.
Her fingers gently pressed, slowly twisted, swept, then picked,
First playing "Rainbow Skirt," then later "Six String."

The heavy strings rumbled like sudden rain,
The thin strings whispered like secret talk.
Rumbling and whispering intertwined in the playing,
Like large and small pearls falling into a jade plate.
Smooth and sweet as orioles singing beneath flowers,
Choked and muffled as spring water flowing under ice.
The icy spring grew cold and sluggish, the strings seemed to freeze;
When frozen tight and blocked, the sound suddenly ceased.

But there arose a hidden sorrow, a dark resentment born;
In that silence, it spoke louder than any voice could do.
Then, abruptly, a silver vase burst, water spurting forth;
Iron-clad cavalry charged out, the clash of swords and spears ringing.
At the end of the song, she drew her plectrum across the center;
With one final strum of four strings, it sounded like tearing silk.
Boats to the east and west fell silent, not a word spoken;
Only the white autumn moon was seen in the middle of the river.

She paused in thought, set down her plectrum among the strings,
Straightened her clothes and rose, composing her expression.
She said she was originally a girl from the capital city,
Living near Xiama Ling, where her family made their home.
At thirteen, she mastered the pipa, becoming skilled;
Her name belonged to the first troupe of the Imperial Music Bureau.
After finishing a piece, even the great musicians admired her skill;
When made up, every beauty envied her grace.

Young nobles from the Five Barrows vied to give her gifts;
For one song, she received an uncountable amount of red silk.
Gold hairpins and silver combs snapped to keep rhythm;
Blood-red skirts were stained with spilled wine during revelry.
Year after year, laughter repeated itself;
Autumn moons and spring breezes passed idly by.
Then her brother went off to war, her aunt died young;
Evenings came and mornings left, her beauty faded away.

Cold and deserted was her doorway, horses rare;
Growing old, she married a merchant who cared for profit more than love.
He valued money over separation, leaving without regret;
Last month, he went to Fuliang to buy tea.
When he left, she guarded the empty boat at the river mouth;
The bright moon surrounded the boat, the river waters cold.
Deep in the night, she suddenly dreamed of her youth’s deeds;
Waking in tears, her rouge-stained face was wet with sorrow.

Hearing the pipa, I had already sighed;
Hearing these words, I groaned again in deep sympathy.
We are both people cast adrift at the ends of the earth;
Meeting now, why should it matter if we knew each other before?
Since last year, I left the imperial capital,
Demoted and ill, lying low in Xunyang City.
Xunyang is remote, devoid of music;
All year round, I hear never the sound of silk and bamboo instruments.

My dwelling is near the Pen River, low land, damp and wet;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo grow around my house.
What sounds do I hear here at dawn and dusk?
Cuckoos crying blood and monkeys wailing mournfully.
On spring nights with blossoms, or autumn nights with the moon,
Often I take wine alone to pour for myself.
Are there no mountain songs and village flutes?
They are harsh and grating, unbearable to listen to.

Tonight, hearing your pipa language,
It is like listening to celestial music; my ears are momentarily enlightened.
Do not refuse to sit again and play one more tune;
For you, I shall compose the "Ballad of the Pipa."

Moved by my words, she stood for a long time;
Then she sat back down, tightened the strings, and played faster.
Mournful and sad, unlike the previous tones;
The whole audience, hearing it again, covered their faces and wept.
Who wept the most among those seated?
The Sima of Jiangzhou, whose blue robe was soaked with tears.

messages.content:
下面是刚才排序第一名的完整译文。原样输出该译文，保留诗行，不改写、不添加说明：
By the banks of the Xun River, I bade a friend farewell at night;
Maple leaves and reed flowers rustled in the autumn chill.
The host dismounted while his guest remained upon the boat;
We raised our cups to drink, but lacked the sound of pipes and strings.
Though drunk, no joy we found, as sorrow marked our parting;
At the moment of farewell, only the moon floated on the vast river’s face.

Suddenly, from over the water, came the notes of a pipa;
The host forgot to return home, the guest forbore to depart.
Following the sound, I quietly asked who played;
The pipa ceased, yet she hesitated, loath to speak.
I moved my boat close by to invite her sight;
I added wine, relit the lamps, and reopened the feast.

After a thousand calls, ten thousand summons, she finally emerged,
Still holding her pipa before her face, half-veiled.
She turned the pegs and plucked a few initial strings;
Before a melody formed, emotion was already there.
Each string veiled low, each note heavy with thought,
As if telling of a life unfulfilled and thwarted.
With downcast brows, her fingers moved freely onward,
Expressing all the boundless matters in her heart.

She lightly gathered, slowly twisted, swept and then picked;
First playing "Rainbow Skirt," then "Six Strings."
The great strings thundered like sudden rainstorm's rush;
The small strings whispered like intimate confessions’ hush.
Thundering and whispering mixed in complex play,
Like large pearls falling into a jade dish, scattering away.

Now clear like orioles singing beneath flowers, swift and smooth;
Now choked like spring water blocked beneath ice, slow and tough.
When cold as ice, the flow became stiff, strings grew silent;
Silence halted when the passage found no way to pass.
Then arose hidden sorrows and buried resentments—
In this stillness, silence surpassed even sound’s expression.

Suddenly, as if a silver vase burst open, water spraying;
Like armored cavalry charging forth, swords clashing, spears ringing.
When the tune ended, she swept her pick across the center;
One sharp cry from four strings, like tearing silk that splits.

East boats and west barges stood in quiet speechlessness;
Only the autumn moon shone white in the river’s heart.
She paused, deep in thought, inserted the pick among the strings,
Straightened her clothes and rose, composing her countenance.

She spoke: "Once I was a girl of the imperial capital,
My home lay near the Shama Ling, where tombs reside.
At thirteen, I mastered the pipa; my skill was renowned,
And ranked first among those trained within the Imperial Music Bureau.
After finishing a piece, even skilled masters admired;
Once dressed up, other beauties envied my grace.

Young men of Five Tombs vied to offer me gifts of cloth;
For one song alone, countless bolts of red gauze were mine.
Gold-inlaid combs shattered keeping time with the beat;
Blood-red silk skirts stained by overturned wine.
Year after year passed amidst laughter and merriment,
Idly spending autumn moons and spring breezes’ fleeting time.

My brother joined the army; my auntie died;
Days fled, nights came, my beauty faded with age.
Few horses remained before my door, visitors scarce;
Aged beyond marriageable years, I wed a merchant’s wife.

He valued profit, slighted separation’s pain;
Last month he went to Fuliang to buy tea, far away.
Since then, I’ve waited here at the river mouth, guarding an empty boat,
Surrounded by bright moonlight, the river waters cold.

Deep in the night, I suddenly dreamed of my youth’s affairs;
In dreams I wept, my makeup tears staining red upon my face."

Hearing the pipa, I had already sighed in sympathy;
Hearing these words, my sighs grew heavier still.
We are both castaways wandering under heaven’s span—
What need is there to have known each other before?

"Since last year I left the imperial capital behind,
Demoted, ill, I lie sick in Xunyang City’s confines.
Xunyang’s land is remote, devoid of music’s charm;
All year round, I hear not silk nor bamboo’s sound.
Near the Pen River lies my dwelling, low and damp;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo encircle my house.

What do I hear between dawn and dusk in this place?
Cuckoos crying blood, apes wailing mournfully.
On spring rivers’ flower mornings or autumn nights’ moonlit hours,
I often take my wine and pour it out alone.

Are there no mountain songs? No village flutes’ simple tune?
Their harsh, grating sounds are difficult to bear to hear.
Tonight, hearing your pipa’s language, pure and sweet,
It is like listening to immortal music; my ears are cleared anew.

Do not refuse to sit again and play another tune;
For you, I shall compose the 'Song of the Pipa.'"

Moved by my words, she stood for a long while in thought;
Then sat back down, pressing strings that played more urgently now.
Melancholy tones unlike the previous ones sounded;
Everyone present, hearing again, wiped their eyes with tears.

Who wept most heavily among those seated there?
The Sima of Jiangzhou, his blue robe soaked through.

