The thin strings whispered soft private powers.
Clamoring, whispering, mixed in intricate play,
Like pearls large and small falling on jade trays.
Smooth as an oriole singing beneath blossoms’ shade;
Choked as a spring bubbling under ice, delayed.
When icy springs froze stiff, the strings ceased their breath;
The silence stopped, yet lingered after death.
A hidden sorrow, a dark resentment born:
At this still moment, silence surpassed tone.

Then suddenly, as silver bottles burst apart,
Water sprayed forth; iron riders charged with darting art;
Swords clashed and spears rang out in battle cry.
When the song ended, she swept the pick across the four,
One sharp sound tearing silk beyond recall.
East boats west skiffs fell silent, awe-struck all;
Only the moon’s white light on mid-river stream.

She pondered long, stuck the pick between strings tight,
Straightened her clothes, composed her face with grace.
"I am," she said, "a girl of the imperial city,
My home lies below Xiama Ridge’s embrace.
At thirteen, I mastered the pipa’s skill;
In the First Section of the Academy, my name stood tall.
When tunes were done, even masters bowed in awe;
When makeup set, beauty envied me before.
Young nobles of Five Mausoleums vied to give;
For one song, bolts of red gauze they would live.
Jeweled hairpins broke keeping time’s beat;
Blood-red skirts stained with spilled wine’s retreat.
Year after year, laughter rose and fell;
Autumn moon and spring wind passed without spell.
My brother joined the army; my aunt died young;
Beauty fades while days and nights are wrung.
Front door grew cold; horse carriages rare;
Growing old, I married a merchant’s wife’s despair.
He values profit more than farewell’s pain;
Last month to Fuliang he went for tea again.
Left behind at the river mouth, guarding empty ship,
Moonlight circles it, waters chill and dip.
Late at night, dreams of youth return to wake;
Crying in sleep, tears trace red down my cheek."

I heard the pipa and sighed deeply already;
Hearing these words, I murmured sadly.
"Both are wanderers fallen from the sky;
Meeting now, why did we know each other?"

Since last year, I left the imperial capital,
Demoted, sickly, lying low in Xunyang’s halt.
Xunyang is remote, lacking music’s voice;
All year round, no pipes nor flutes rejoice.
Living near the Pen River, land low and wet,
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo around us set.
What do I hear at dawn and dusk here?
Cuckoos bleeding cries, apes moaning fear.
Spring rivers, flower mornings, autumn moon nights,
Often take wine alone, pouring solitary bites.
Are there mountain songs and village flute sounds?
Ugly noises grating, harsh and unsound.
Tonight hearing your pipa’s language sweet,
Like heavenly music, ears made clean and neat.
Do not refuse to sit and play another tune;
I shall compose for you this Ballad of the Lute.

Moved by my words, she stood a long while;
Sat back, tightened strings, playing wilder style.
Mournful sounds unlike those played before;
Everyone present covered faces, shedding tear.
Whose eyes shed most among the seated folk?
The Jiangzhou Prefect, blue robe soaked.

