**The Song of the Pipa**

By the Xunyang River, I bid a friend farewell at night;
Maple leaves and reed flowers shiver in the autumn chill.
I dismount my horse while he awaits his boat;
We raise our cups to drink, yet lack the music’s note.
Drunkenness brings no joy as parting turns to grief;
At farewell time, the moon sinks deep within the river’s leaf.
Suddenly, from over the water, the pipa’s sound is heard;
The host forgets to return, the guest delays his word.
Seeking the source, we ask who plays in the dark;
The pipa stops, she hesitates before she speaks her mark.
We move our boats close by to invite her sight;
More wine is poured, lamps turned up, the feast renewed tonight.
After a thousand calls and ten thousand shouts, she comes;
Still holding the pipa, half her face remains undone.
She twists the pegs and plucks two or three strings;
Before the tune is formed, emotion already springs.
Each string suppressed, each note filled with thought;
It seems to tell of all ambitions dearly brought to naught.
With lowered brows, fingers continue their play;
Telling out the infinite sorrows of her day.
Lightly gathering, slowly twisting, brushing, then picking;
First the *Rainbow Skirt*, then the *Six Flutes* keep ticking.
The thick string roars like sudden rain in haste;
The thin string whispers like lovers’ secret taste.
Roaring and whispering mixed in tangled play;
Like large and small pearls falling on jade plates away.
Clear sweet as orioles singing beneath the flowers;
Muffled choked as spring water under ice-bound hours.
Cold ice blocks the flow, the strings freeze tight;
When frozen still, no passage found, sound halts its flight.
Hidden sorrow and secret hate are born anew;
In this silence, more than sound, the heart breaks through.
Then suddenly a silver bottle bursts, water spurts and sprays;
Iron cavalry charges out, swords clash and guns blaze.
The song ends; she draws the pick across the center’s grace;
One final stroke on four strings tears silk in space.
Boats east and west fall silent, speechless in the air;
Only the white moon of autumn on the river’s mirror bare.
Lost in thought, she puts the pick among the strings;
Adjusts her clothes, rises, composing what it brings.
She says: "My home was once in the Imperial City’s height;
I lived near Shrimp Frog Tomb, in old days’ light.
At thirteen, I mastered the pipa’s art so fine;
Ranked first among those trained in the Academy’s line.
After I finished playing, even masters were won;
When dressed, every beauty envied me as one.
Young men of Five Tombs competed to give gifts with gold;
For one song, red silks counted beyond told.
Hairpins of silver beat the rhythm till they broke;
Blood-red skirts stained with spilled wine, no joke.
This year of laughter, next year’s joy again;
Autumn moons and spring breezes passed like idle rain.
My brother joined the army, my auntie died;
Days fade, nights come, youth’s color subsided.
Before my door, cold loneliness, horses rarely stay;
Growing old, I married a merchant far away.
He values profit high, regards parting low;
Last month he went to Liangfu for tea’s slow glow.
Leaving me alone at the river mouth to guard an empty ship;
Moonlight circles the vessel, water cold and deep.
Late at night, I dreamt of things when I was young;
Dreaming, I cried, my makeup run with tears that clung."
Hearing the pipa, I had sighed already long;
Hearing these words, I sigh again with deeper wrong.
Same fate exiled to the ends of earth we share;
Why need we have known each other in the past’s care?
Since last year, I left the capital’s imperial throne;
Demoted, sick, lying in Xunyang city alone.
Xunyang is remote, no music here to hear;
All year round, no bamboo flutes strike the ear.
Living near the Pen River, land low and wet;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboos around my cottage set.
What do I hear morning, evening, in this place?
Cuckoos crying blood, apes wailing in disgrace.
Spring rivers, flower mornings, autumn moonlit nights;
Often take my wine, pouring it alone in sights.
Are there no mountain songs and village pipes?
They are harsh and noisy, unpleasing to wise ears.
Tonight, hearing your pipa’s tale of woes;
It is like hearing fairy music, my ears unclosed.
Do not refuse to sit again and play another strain;
For you, I shall compose this *Song of the Pipa*’s pain.
Moved by my words, she stood for a long while;
Returned to seat, tightened strings, tempo piled.
Sad sounds unlike those played before arose;
Full house heard again, tears in silence flows.
Who in the audience wept the most profound?
The blue robe of Jiangzhou’s司马 was soaked and drowned.

