{
"translation": "Ten years of life and death, each lost to the other; / Without effort, I cannot forget. / A lonely grave lies a thousand miles away; / Where can I speak my grief? / Even if we met again, you would not know me: / Dust on my face, hair frost-white as silver. / Last night in a dream I suddenly returned home; / By the small window, you were combing your hair. / We looked at each other, speechless, / Only tears flowed down in streams. / I know where my heart breaks year after year: / Under the bright moon, on the short pine-covered ridge."
}

{
"translation": "Ten years apart, the living and dead are both lost in vast oblivion; Without deliberate thought, I cannot forget. A lonely grave lies a thousand miles away, Where there is no place to pour out this desolate grief. Even if we were to meet again, you might not recognize me: My face is dust-stained, my temples frost-white like hair. Last night, in a dim dream, I suddenly returned home. At the small window of the pavilion, you were just dressing your hair. We looked at each other and said nothing, Only tears streamed down a thousand rows. I foresee that the place where my heart will break year after year Is on a moonlit night, at the mound of short pine trees."
}

{"translation":"Ten years have passed, separating life from death in boundless mist;
Though I strive not to think of you, your memory stays.
A lonely grave lies a thousand li away, where I find no place to share my desolation.
Even if we were to meet again, perhaps you would not know me—
My face dust-covered, my temples white as frost.

Last night, in dreams, I suddenly returned home;
By the small window, you were doing your hair.
We gazed at each other, speechless, with only tears falling like rain.
I imagine that year after year, where my heart breaks most is there:
Under the bright moon, on the hill of short pines."}

