messages.content:
The nation is shattered, yet mountains and rivers remain;
In spring the city grows thick with weeds and trees.
Grieved by the times, flowers shed tears;
Hating partings, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires have burned for three months on end;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand golds.
I scratch my white hair, which grows ever thinner,
Barely able to hold a hairpin anymore.

The Nation Broken

The nation falls, yet mountains and rivers remain;
In the city’s spring, grass and trees grow deep.
Moved by the times, flowers bring tears to my eyes;
Hating partings, birds startle my heart.
Beacon fires have blazed for three months on end;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand gold.
I scratch my white hair, now grown thinner;
It can no longer hold even a hairpin.

