Beacon fires have burned for three months on end;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand golds.
I scratch my white hair, which grows ever thinner,
Barely able to hold a hairpin anymore.

The nation lies in ruins, yet mountains and rivers remain;
In the city’s spring, grass and trees grow deep and dense.
Grieving for the times, I see flowers shed tears;
Hating separation, I hear birds startle my heart.
War beacons have burned fiercely for three months straight;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand gold pieces.
I scratch my white-haired head, finding fewer strands each day,
So thin now that they can hardly hold a hairpin at all.

The wind is sharp, the sky vast; gibbons wail in grief.
Islets clear, sands white; birds circle back.

Endless leaves fall rustling down;
Boundless Yangtze rolls on ceaselessly.

Ten thousand miles of autumn sorrow, ever a sojourner’s lot;
A lifetime of illness, alone I ascend this terrace.

Hardship and bitter regret have thickened frost upon my temples;
In destitution, I have newly ceased to raise my cloudy wine cup.

A new song, a cup of wine;
Last year's weather, the old pavilion and terrace.
When will the setting sun return?

Helplessly, the flowers fall away;
As if recognized before, the swallows come back.
Alone I linger on the fragrant path in the small garden.

After the fresh rain, the empty mountains lie still;
As evening falls, a chill of autumn fills the air.
Bright moonlight streams through pines upon the hill;
Clear springs cascade over stones with gentle care.

Laughter among the bamboo: washerwomen return to shore;
Lotus leaves part and stir as fishing boats glide down.
Though spring’s fragrance fades, let nature take its course once more;
For here, the noble wanderer may freely rest his gown.

The wind is sharp, the sky high, apes wail in sorrow;
Islets clear, sands white, birds circle in flight.
Endless falling leaves rustle down;
Unending Yangtze rolls onward.
Grief of autumn spanning ten thousand miles, I am ever a sojourner;
A life of sickness, now old and frail, I climb the terrace alone.
Hardships and bitter regrets have frosted my temples;
In decline, I have just ceased to raise my cup of turbid wine.

I lean long against the high tower, where the breeze whispers thin;
Gazing far into spring’s sorrow, it rises darkly from heaven’s rim.
Grass hues and misty light fade in the dying sun’s last glow,
Wordless, who understands my heart as I lean on the rail below?

I plan to cast off restraint and seek a drunken trance;
Facing wine, singing loudly, forced joy lacks romance.
My clothes grow loose, yet never do I regret this wear;
For her sake alone, I let myself waste away in despair.

