Leaves without limit fall rustling down;
The Yangtze River flows on without end, rolling ceaselessly onward.
For ten thousand miles I sorrow at autumn’s chill, forever a wanderer;
In my hundred years, racked by illness, I climb alone onto this terrace.
Hardships and bitter regrets have whitened my temples like frost;
Downtrodden and worn, I’ve newly stopped drinking from my turbid wine cup.

