Singing to the wine, forced joy still lacks flavor.
My sash loosens gradually, yet I never regret it;
For her sake, I gladly bear this withered form."}

{
"translation": "The wind has stilled, dust holds the scent of flowers now all gone;
By midday’s glow, I’m weary, too spent to comb my hair.
Things remain as they were, but people are not; all joy is done—
I long to speak, yet tears flow before a word can be shared.

I’ve heard that at Double Creek, spring still lingers in its grace,
And so I plan to drift away in a light boat’s gentle sway.
But I fear those tiny boats upon the creek’s embrace
Could never bear the weight of this sorrow within me today."
}

