In the city’s spring, grass and trees grow deep.
Grieving for the times, flowers seem to weep;
Hating separation, birds startle the heart.
For three months, beacon fires have burned on;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand gold.
I scratch my white hair, growing ever shorter,
So sparse that it can barely hold a pin.

messages.content:
I lean against the high tower, waiting as the gentle breeze blows;
Gazing far, my spring sorrow rises darkly from the horizon.

In the fading light, grass and misty haze merge together;
Silent, who can understand my heart as I rest on the railing?

I resolve to drink freely, seeking a drunken escape;
Singing while drinking, forced joy tastes hollow and bland.

Yet though my belt grows loose, I regret nothing at all—
For her sake, I would wither away even unto death.

I lean against the high tower, waiting as the gentle breeze blows;
Gazing far, my spring sorrow rises dimly from the horizon.

In the fading light, grass and misty haze merge together;
Wordless, who comprehends my longing by the rail?

With wild abandon, I aim for drunkenness;
Singing while drinking, forced joy tastes hollow and bland.

Though my belt grows loose, I regret nothing at all—
It is worth it to grow pale and thin for her.

I lean against the high tower, feeling the fine breeze;
Gazing afar, my spring sorrow rises dimly from the sky's edge.

In the fading light, grass and misty haze blend;
Silent, who understands my mood as I rest on the railing?

I plan to act wildly, seeking but one intoxication;
Singing with wine, forced joy tastes hollow and bland.

Though my belt grows loose, I regret not at all—
For her sake, I willingly grow pale and thin.

