Silent I stand—what can be done?
No one knows my heart's sad ways.

I'd cast off restraint to seek one drunken bliss,
To sing and drink, yet find no ease;
Forced joy tastes bitter, void of kiss,
A hollow mask amidst the trees.

My belt grows loose, but I don't rue,
Though wasted thin by love's long siege;
For Her I fade, day after new,
Regretting nothing time has wrung."}

{"translation":"Leaning against the high tower, the breeze is fine and thin;
Gazing far into spring sorrow, it rises dimly from the horizon's edge.
In the grassy hues of smoky light and fading sun,
Silent—who understands my longing at the railing?

I resolve to seek one drunkenness in wild abandon,
Singing to wine, forcing joy that tastes hollow.
Though my belt loosens as I waste away, I never repent;
For her sake, I gladly bear this haggard decline."}

{
 "translation": "I live at the head of the Yangtze,
You live at its tail.
Day by day I long for you, yet never see your face;
We share the same river water.
When will this stream cease?
When will this sorrow end?
May your heart be as mine,
And I shall not betray this longing."
}

