The nation is broken, yet mountains and rivers remain;
In spring, the city’s grass and trees grow thick and deep.
Grieving for the times, flowers bring tears to my eyes;
Hating parting, birds’ cries startle my heart.
Beacon fires have burned for three months on end;
A letter from home would be worth ten thousand in gold.
I scratch my white hair, which grows ever shorter,
Until it fails to hold even a single hairpin.

